<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hudson's Bay Company</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1670">1670</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hudson's Bay Company was chartered by Charles II in 1670 with a monopoly of trade in all lands draining into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-bay">Hudson Bay</a>, a territory called Rupert's Land. It ran the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cree">Cree</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assiniboine">Assiniboine</a> and other peoples from posts on the bay and later across the west, merging with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-company">North West Company</a> in 1821. It sold Rupert's Land to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> in 1870.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company.jpg" alt="Hudson's Bay Company" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hudson%27s_Bay_Company_Official_Logo_2013.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Pemmican War, men of the <b>Hudson's Bay Company</b> and the North West Company fought a violent confrontation near modern-day Winnipeg, also called the Seven Oaks Massacre.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-19">19 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early 18th century they acquired horses from the south and guns from Cree middlemen trading with the <b>Hudson's Bay Company</b>, and the Siksika, Kainai and Piikani emerged as an allied military power.</blockquote><a href="/state/blackfoot-confederacy">Blackfoot Confederacy</a></li><li><blockquote>When gold was found on the Fraser River in 1858, some 30,000 miners, mostly from California, poured into territory held only by <b>Hudson's Bay Company</b> posts.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-british-columbia-1858-1866">Colony of British Columbia (1858-1866)</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Oregon Treaty of 1846 left Britain only the lands north of the 49th parallel, the Crown made Vancouver Island a colony in 1849 and leased it to the <b>Hudson's Bay Company</b> on condition that it bring settlers.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-vancouver-island">Colony of Vancouver Island</a></li><li><blockquote>The Cree and the Assiniboine, or Nakoda, had long been allies, and in the 18th century they became the main middlemen between the <b>Hudson's Bay Company</b> posts on Hudson Bay and peoples of the interior.</blockquote><a href="/state/iron-confederacy">Iron Confederacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1869 the <b>Hudson's Bay Company</b> agreed to sell Rupert's Land to Canada without consulting the people of the Red River Colony.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-assiniboia">Provisional Government of Assiniboia</a></li></ul></section>
